A||||11||deceler8||HELP...Can't open my decklid...||||11||
Z||000000||deceler8||07-18-2000||01:59 PM||||Out of nowhere comes this problem with my decklid...lock worked great, never a problem, now it won't open. Key will not turn at all in the lock.<P>Yes it is the right key, no I don't have a remote release, and yes, I'm turning it the right way (sorry, just anticipating my wife's responses)...<P>Do I have any good options here ? Tried my spare key and it doesn't work either. Also sprayed some WD-40 in...no go.<P>Hope it's not Sawzall time...||||reg||11
Z||000001||My7Fieros||07-18-2000||02:19 PM||||EEK! Sounds like youve got a prob on your hands! WD40 dosnt do alot for locks. Try using lock graphite...you can get it at any hardware store..its a powdery substance that you "squirt" (for lack of a better term) into the key hole. Its really the only thing I can think of off the top of my head. If that dosnt work, find a local criminal and have him punch the lock out...I see it everyday!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000002||triker||07-18-2000||06:24 PM||||I had the same prob.  Went to a lock smith and he told me to drill it out with a 1/4 in bit.  You'll have to buy a new lock from GM and have it keyed by a locksmith. Was an easy fix but kind of expensive . ||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||07-18-2000||06:46 PM||||Those Briggs & Stratton sidebar locks aren't easy to get open.  Remember that if you exert turning pressure before the key's all the way in, it will hang up on you.  If key is loose in the hole, try turning the wrong way (CCW), then the right way (CW) after each try at repositioning the key in the hole.  Sorry if this sounds like a "wife" response  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> .  <P>Maybe a tumbler spring failed so it doesn't come up to the notches in the key.  You could go in with a wire and see if each tumbler comes up as you withdraw the wire.<P>But even if that's it, what do you do? Invert the car?  Now we're back to drilling out the cylinder ...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">    Good Luck.     ||||reg||13
Z||000004||deceler8||07-19-2000||12:41 PM||||Well...went to a locksmith and he said the lock cylinder is okay since the key turns a little...says the lid is jammed or the latch is broken.<P>Anyone have any ideas now or is the locksmith full of it ?<P>There can't be anything caught in the latch since the trunk is empty...this thing worked great until day before yesterday.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||07-19-2000||01:08 PM||||Inset key <B>almost</B> all the way into the lock cylinder (not all the way in), turn gently CW and observe how far it turns.  Next push key rest of the way in, back off (CCW) slightly and see if it will turn further CW now.<P>If lock turns farther than before, you know the cylinder is releasing - and your locksmith is on the right track.         ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Monkeyman||07-19-2000||01:08 PM||||Try pushing down on the decklid as you operate the key.  That might release some of the pressure on the latch.  It's worth a try.||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||07-19-2000||03:01 PM||||I've tried all these things with no success...even went to a dealer and he said to try a crowbar...some people should never be afraid to say "I Don't Know..."<P>Looks like the time for finesse has passed...would I be able to operate the latch manually if the lock cylinder was removed ?<P>Sorry about all the dumb questions...of course my service manual is locked in the trunk. Go figure.||||reg||1
Z||000008||HarryG||07-19-2000||03:24 PM||||Once the cylinder is out, find a long blade<BR>screwdriver.  You'll need one with at least 10 in. overall length, and a flat blade about 1/4" wide.  First time, you'll need a flashlight to see the target, which is a slot in the mechanism.<BR>Just go straight into the cylinder hole, and when you find the slot, just turn the screwdriver and bingo! Once you get onto this, it's almost as fast as using the key!<P>P.S. Learned this one at the local boneyard since lots of the cars there have "punched" locks.||||reg||12
Z||000009||deceler8||07-19-2000||03:53 PM||||Okay...what's the best way to get the cylinder out ?<P>I'd like to be able to put another lock cylinder on, so I don't want to destroy whatever the cylinder mounts to.<P>I just want to get the damm thing opened without spending a fortune or needing to find/paint a new decklid.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||07-19-2000||10:45 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by deceler8:<BR>[B]Okay...what's the best way to get the cylinder out ?<BR>try the pushing down while you try to turn it like said above, but work the key in and out slightly as you turn it. If dont work try turning key and pulling up on lid. Sounds to me like you might have caught something like a part of a rag or somethig when you shut it. If it still wont work us a 1/4 drill bit into the center of the lock till it falls apart. Once in a while you can get a trunk lock with the key in a junkyard for almost nothing. all GM trunk locks are same except for the arm inside and youll be able to use the old one from yours, its held on with a spring when youve got the lock out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||deceler8||07-20-2000||07:04 PM||||Well, took a 9/32 tool steel drill and my hammer drill and 30 seconds later it was open. That drill went through that lock like a knife through butter...not very reassuring.<P>I should have known this would be easier than I thought...had a '79 Cutlass that was broken into three times and stolen once...using the key was almost the hard way to get into that car.<P>Now that it's done, I would have felt really stupid paying a locksmith $80 to drill out and replace the lock.<P>Thanks again for all your suggestions.||||reg||1
